typical seqlocks:

--------------
writer():
--------------
lock() // protect against other writers
atomic_incr(version, 1)

change shared data (data)

atomic_incr(version, 1)
unlock()

--------------
reader():
--------------
outputdata
do {
      do {
   	v0 = load(version);
      } while(v0 & 1); // if its an odd number dont enter ... writer is modifying

      copy_from_to(data, outputdata)

      v1 = load(version)
} while( v1 != v0)

return outputdata;

--------------------------------------------------------
 My Thoughts:
--------------------------------------------------------
--> The odd protection is to ensure readers dont go and trash
cache lines that the writers are modifying since readers will
have to rollback anyways.

--> My argument being with sufficiently large #readers and a moderate sized
data odds are against you ... readers will miss that small lil odd check.
So this has a scalability problem for both large #readers and large data structs.
Meaning you ll get that cacheline shitstorm anyways!!

--> Also, I m not entirely convinced reader doing plain load of 
version is guaranteed to get the latest write. Can processors cheat?
As you can see if he returns old data and old version he is still TLO
and causally consistent. So you could land up getting stale but consistent
readers ... which is ok I guess

--> Oh and the biggest issue being this is what I call a SNAPSHOT BASED READER LOCK!!
Meaning you can get a consistent snapshot of the data but since it involves rollback,
you may not hold this reader lock to produce other side effects of your own on other
data structures!!


